1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,120
Speculative decoding starts with a small

2
00:00:02,639 --> 00:00:07,520
model guessing the next few tokens. And

3
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:10,080
because that model is small, it produces

4
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:12,080
those guesses quickly. The large model

5
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:14,480
then takes the whole batch of guesses

6
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:16,640
and checks them in one pass. When the

7
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:18,960
guesses hold up, that single pass

8
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:21,199
returns several tokens instead of one,

9
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:24,160
and it reads the weights exactly once to

10
00:00:21,199 --> 00:00:26,800
do it. Nothing in the last episode

11
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:29,760
worked that way. batching, [music]

12
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:31,760
continuous scheduling, and paged caches

13
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:34,399
all worked by putting more requests

14
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:36,399
behind each read of the weights. None of

15
00:00:34,399 --> 00:00:39,280
that helps a machine that is running one

16
00:00:36,399 --> 00:00:43,840
request on its own. To produce a single

17
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:46,559
token, the GPU reads all 140 GB of a 70

18
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:49,600
billion parameter model. Then it reads

19
00:00:46,559 --> 00:00:52,480
the same 140 GB again for the next

20
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:56,079
token. And the arithmetic units sit

21
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:57,920
nearly idle during the reads.

22
00:00:56,079 --> 00:01:00,640
So each read of the weights has to

23
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:03,600
produce more than one token. Each

24
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:06,720
technique ahead does one of two things.

25
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:09,040
It either moves fewer bytes per token or

26
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:12,159
it gets more work out of the bytes that

27
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:14,320
already moved. Speculative decoding is

28
00:01:12,159 --> 00:01:17,200
the kind that gets more work out of the

29
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:19,600
same bytes. The long context ideas that

30
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:23,200
we'll discuss later on mostly move fewer

31
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:24,960
of them. First let's look at speculative

32
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:27,280
decoding.

33
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:29,520
The GPU reads the model's weights once

34
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:31,840
per forward pass, no matter how many

35
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:34,079
token positions are in that pass.

36
00:01:31,840 --> 00:01:37,680
Ordinary decode has one position. So

37
00:01:34,079 --> 00:01:39,840
those 140 GB produce a single token.

38
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:43,200
Verification puts five positions in one

39
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:46,560
pass. So a single read of those 140 GB

40
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:48,960
covers all five. The small model writes

41
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:51,360
its guesses the normal way, one token at

42
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:54,399
a time, feeding [music] each one back in

43
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:57,600
to produce the next. Five guesses take

44
00:01:54,399 --> 00:01:59,920
five of its own forward passes. The

45
00:01:57,600 --> 00:02:03,040
small model is the drafter and the large

46
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:05,200
model that checks it is the target. The

47
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:07,280
2022 speculative decoding paper found

48
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:10,000
the drafter usually works best at about

49
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:12,319
100 times smaller than the target. At

50
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,080
that size, five drafter passes move

51
00:02:12,319 --> 00:02:18,640
roughly 7 GB in total, next to 140 for

52
00:02:16,080 --> 00:02:20,720
one pass of the large model.

53
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:23,200
The large model then takes the text so

54
00:02:20,720 --> 00:02:26,720
far with those five guesses added to the

55
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:29,200
end. It runs one pass over the whole

56
00:02:26,720 --> 00:02:31,760
sequence [music] and at every position

57
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:34,480
it produces its own distribution over

58
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:36,959
what the next token should be there.

59
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:39,920
Attention is causal. So each position

60
00:02:36,959 --> 00:02:42,080
sees only the tokens before it. So the

61
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:44,560
prediction at the third guess depends

62
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:47,040
only on the first two which is exactly

63
00:02:44,560 --> 00:02:49,680
the information the large model has when

64
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:51,920
it writes that position itself.

65
00:02:49,680 --> 00:02:53,680
The large model then compares each guess

66
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:56,239
against the prediction sitting at its

67
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:58,560
position. It keeps the guesses it agrees

68
00:02:56,239 --> 00:03:01,360
with and throws away everything from the

69
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:03,840
first disagreement onward. If all five

70
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:06,080
survive, the pass gives back a sixth

71
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:07,840
token for free because the model has

72
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:09,440
already made a prediction at the last

73
00:03:07,840 --> 00:03:11,840
position.

74
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:14,959
It does mean that we have five times the

75
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:16,879
arithmetic compared to a single token.

76
00:03:14,959 --> 00:03:19,280
But arithmetic was never the bottleneck

77
00:03:16,879 --> 00:03:21,599
for decode. The paper assumes those

78
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:25,040
extra positions run in parallel on idle

79
00:03:21,599 --> 00:03:27,360
compute and add no wall clock time. The

80
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:30,080
tokens that survive were still written

81
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:31,840
by the weaker model. The obvious worry

82
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:33,519
in this case is that the output gets

83
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:35,599
worse.

84
00:03:33,519 --> 00:03:38,640
Whether the output gets worse depends on

85
00:03:35,599 --> 00:03:40,879
how the guesses are accepted. One option

86
00:03:38,640 --> 00:03:43,280
is to keep a guess whenever the drafter

87
00:03:40,879 --> 00:03:45,200
was confident, but that means sampling

88
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:47,519
from the small model and the small

89
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:49,920
model's distribution is not the one the

90
00:03:47,519 --> 00:03:53,120
customer asked for. The rule in the

91
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:55,280
original paper avoids that completely.

92
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:58,239
It works through the drafted tokens one

93
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:59,920
at a time. The large model has its own

94
00:03:58,239 --> 00:04:02,400
probability for the token the small

95
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:04,480
model proposed and the small model had

96
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:06,959
one too. When the large model's

97
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:09,840
probability is at least as high, the

98
00:04:06,959 --> 00:04:12,080
token is accepted outright. When it is

99
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:14,080
lower, the token is accepted with

100
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:17,040
probability equal to the large models

101
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:19,440
number divided by the small models. For

102
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:22,880
example, a token the drafter rated at

103
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:26,240
60% and the target rated at 30% has a

104
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:28,639
50% probability of being accepted.

105
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:32,000
A rejected token is replaced rather than

106
00:04:28,639 --> 00:04:33,600
dropped. The large model samples the

107
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:35,680
replacement from the difference between

108
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:37,840
the two distributions.

109
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:40,400
It takes its own probability for every

110
00:04:37,840 --> 00:04:42,720
token in the vocabulary, subtracts the

111
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:45,583
drafters, clips anything negative to

112
00:04:42,720 --> 00:04:47,759
zero, and renormalizes what is left.

113
00:04:45,583 --> 00:04:49,680
[music] That leftover distribution has

114
00:04:47,759 --> 00:04:52,320
weight only where the target wanted a

115
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:54,560
token more than the drafter did.

116
00:04:52,320 --> 00:04:56,880
Accepting covers the probability the two

117
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:58,880
models already agreed on. The

118
00:04:56,880 --> 00:05:01,520
replacement covers exactly the amount

119
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:03,840
the drafter was shot. Put them together

120
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:06,639
and the targets distribution is back

121
00:05:03,840 --> 00:05:08,800
token for token. Tokens produced this

122
00:05:06,639 --> 00:05:12,080
way have exactly the same distribution

123
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:13,759
as tokens sampled from the target alone.

124
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:16,720
Nothing in that argument depends on the

125
00:05:13,759 --> 00:05:18,720
drafter being good. A bad drafter simply

126
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:21,280
gets rejected more often which makes it

127
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:23,600
slower and cannot make the output wrong.

128
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:25,759
Later methods do loosen this rule to get

129
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:28,960
more guesses accepted and they give up

130
00:05:25,759 --> 00:05:30,800
the guarantee when they do. The 2022

131
00:05:28,960 --> 00:05:33,600
paper measured a speed up of two to

132
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:37,120
three times on T5XXL with identical

133
00:05:33,600 --> 00:05:39,840
outputs. A second paper from deep mind

134
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:42,000
derived the same rule independently. It

135
00:05:39,840 --> 00:05:45,600
reported two to two and a half times on

136
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:47,600
chinchilla a 70 billion parameter model.

137
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:49,919
The targets distribution held to within

138
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:52,880
hardware numeric.

139
00:05:49,919 --> 00:05:54,479
So the drafter's quality never touches

140
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:56,320
correctness.

141
00:05:54,479 --> 00:05:58,560
All it changes is how many guesses

142
00:05:56,320 --> 00:06:02,000
survive each pass and that is where the

143
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:04,400
speed up comes from. The ordinary way to

144
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:07,280
produce three tokens is three trips

145
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:11,199
through the large model. Each trip reads

146
00:06:07,280 --> 00:06:13,120
all 140 GB. So the three of them move

147
00:06:11,199 --> 00:06:15,600
420.

148
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:19,120
A speculative step produces those same

149
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:21,919
three tokens with one read of 140 plus

150
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:23,919
the 7 GB the drafter moved for its five

151
00:06:21,919 --> 00:06:27,840
guesses.

152
00:06:23,919 --> 00:06:31,039
147 against 420 is a factor of just

153
00:06:27,840 --> 00:06:33,280
under three. So the speed up follows the

154
00:06:31,039 --> 00:06:35,199
acceptance length. The average number of

155
00:06:33,280 --> 00:06:38,000
drafted tokens that survive each

156
00:06:35,199 --> 00:06:40,240
verification step. The real speed up

157
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:43,120
always lands a little under that because

158
00:06:40,240 --> 00:06:46,400
the drafter adds 7 GB to every weight

159
00:06:43,120 --> 00:06:48,319
read. One pass of the large model also

160
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:51,360
has a ceiling on how many extra

161
00:06:48,319 --> 00:06:53,600
positions it can check. Verification is

162
00:06:51,360 --> 00:06:55,840
close to free only while the large model

163
00:06:53,600 --> 00:06:58,160
has compute sitting idle. During

164
00:06:55,840 --> 00:07:00,800
ordinary decode, nearly all of it is

165
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:03,120
idle. The more requests you put behind a

166
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:06,479
single weight read, the less room is

167
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:08,960
left for those five extra positions.

168
00:07:06,479 --> 00:07:11,840
Speculative decoding helps most on a

169
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:13,919
machine serving a single user and least

170
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:15,680
on a machine already packed with other

171
00:07:13,919 --> 00:07:17,919
requests.

172
00:07:15,680 --> 00:07:20,080
Every method that we'll discuss after

173
00:07:17,919 --> 00:07:22,240
this tries to raise that acceptance

174
00:07:20,080 --> 00:07:24,240
length and some drop the drafter

175
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:26,560
altogether.

176
00:07:24,240 --> 00:07:29,039
That drafter is a second model running

177
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:31,280
beside the target. Its weights sit in

178
00:07:29,039 --> 00:07:33,840
memory beside the targets and it needs

179
00:07:31,280 --> 00:07:36,160
retraining whenever the target changes.

180
00:07:33,840 --> 00:07:38,720
The two models also have to share one

181
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:42,000
vocabulary because verification compares

182
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:44,240
their probabilities token for token. How

183
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:46,800
closely the two agree decides how many

184
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:48,800
guesses survive each pass. So a drafter

185
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:51,199
that has drifted away from the target

186
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:54,160
slows everything down.

187
00:07:51,199 --> 00:07:56,639
Medusa from 2024 removes [music] that

188
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:59,680
second model. Instead of a second

189
00:07:56,639 --> 00:08:02,319
network, it adds extra decoding heads to

190
00:07:59,680 --> 00:08:04,800
the model already running. Each head is

191
00:08:02,319 --> 00:08:07,599
a small output layer reading the same

192
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:09,840
hidden state the model just produced.

193
00:08:07,599 --> 00:08:12,400
The first head guesses the token two

194
00:08:09,840 --> 00:08:16,400
positions ahead. The second head three

195
00:08:12,400 --> 00:08:19,199
positions ahead and so on all at once.

196
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:21,520
But nothing links one head to the next.

197
00:08:19,199 --> 00:08:23,759
The head guessing three positions ahead

198
00:08:21,520 --> 00:08:26,400
has no idea what the head before it

199
00:08:23,759 --> 00:08:29,440
settled on. A single chain of guesses

200
00:08:26,400 --> 00:08:31,759
would usually break early. Medusa takes

201
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:33,599
the top few candidates from each head

202
00:08:31,759 --> 00:08:36,080
and combines them into a tree of

203
00:08:33,599 --> 00:08:38,159
possible continuations.

204
00:08:36,080 --> 00:08:40,719
The model verifies the whole tree in one

205
00:08:38,159 --> 00:08:43,519
forward pass. An attention mask lets

206
00:08:40,719 --> 00:08:46,080
each token see only its own ancestors,

207
00:08:43,519 --> 00:08:49,360
never its siblings. With the backbone

208
00:08:46,080 --> 00:08:51,279
left frozen, Medusa 1 reports over 2.2

209
00:08:49,360 --> 00:08:55,040
times speed up without compromising

210
00:08:51,279 --> 00:08:57,279
generation quality. Medusa 2 fine-tunes

211
00:08:55,040 --> 00:09:01,040
the whole model along with the heads and

212
00:08:57,279 --> 00:09:04,320
reaches 2.3 to 3.6 times.

213
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:06,320
Medusa also changed the acceptance test.

214
00:09:04,320 --> 00:09:08,320
The paper argues that matching the

215
00:09:06,320 --> 00:09:10,480
distribution of the original model is

216
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:12,959
typically unnecessary.

217
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:15,200
A drafted token survives whenever the

218
00:09:12,959 --> 00:09:18,080
target's own probability for it clears a

219
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:20,160
threshold. That threshold drops when the

220
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:22,560
target is uncertain because in a

221
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:24,240
genuinely open spot, many tokens are

222
00:09:22,560 --> 00:09:26,720
reasonable.

223
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:28,880
More guesses survive and in exchange,

224
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:32,000
the output is no longer guaranteed to

225
00:09:28,880 --> 00:09:34,000
match what the target alone produces.

226
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:36,320
The heads still guess several positions

227
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:38,800
ahead from a single snapshot of the

228
00:09:36,320 --> 00:09:41,040
model state taken before any of these

229
00:09:38,800 --> 00:09:43,519
guesses were made.

230
00:09:41,040 --> 00:09:46,320
Eagle is a family of drafting methods

231
00:09:43,519 --> 00:09:48,399
from 2024 and it changes what the

232
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:50,240
drafter predicts.

233
00:09:48,399 --> 00:09:52,560
Instead of guessing the next token,

234
00:09:50,240 --> 00:09:55,120
Eagle One predicts the target model's

235
00:09:52,560 --> 00:09:57,600
own internal vector at its second to top

236
00:09:55,120 --> 00:10:00,080
layer, it turns that vector into a token

237
00:09:57,600 --> 00:10:02,480
afterwards. Those internal vectors

238
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:04,320
change smoothly from one position to the

239
00:10:02,480 --> 00:10:06,480
next, which [music] makes them easier to

240
00:10:04,320 --> 00:10:09,600
predict than a token choice that jumps

241
00:10:06,480 --> 00:10:11,519
between discrete options. It also leaves

242
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:14,397
the distribution of the generated text

243
00:10:11,519 --> 00:10:16,720
unchanged, so the output stays exact.

244
00:10:14,397 --> 00:10:20,480
[music] Eagle one arranges its guesses

245
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:23,040
into a tree the same way Medusa does.

246
00:10:20,480 --> 00:10:25,200
Eagle 2 leaves the drafting alone and

247
00:10:23,040 --> 00:10:27,519
changes the shape of that tree. Whether

248
00:10:25,200 --> 00:10:30,240
a guess gets accepted depends heavily on

249
00:10:27,519 --> 00:10:33,200
context. A tree with a fixed shape

250
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:36,640
wastess branches in easy contexts and

251
00:10:33,200 --> 00:10:38,480
runs short of them in hard ones. Eagle 2

252
00:10:36,640 --> 00:10:40,320
grows the tree where the drafter is

253
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:43,360
confident and prunes it where the

254
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:46,560
drafter is not. That is another 20 to

255
00:10:43,360 --> 00:10:48,800
40% over eagle 1, still without changing

256
00:10:46,560 --> 00:10:52,480
the output distribution.

257
00:10:48,800 --> 00:10:54,880
Eagle 3 reverses the original idea. It

258
00:10:52,480 --> 00:10:56,959
goes back to predicting tokens directly,

259
00:10:54,880 --> 00:10:59,040
but it feeds the drafter a mixture of

260
00:10:56,959 --> 00:11:02,000
internal vectors taken from several of

261
00:10:59,040 --> 00:11:04,000
the targets layers instead of just one.

262
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:06,480
that removed a ceiling on how much the

263
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:09,760
drafter improves as it trains on more

264
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:11,279
data. The paper reports up to 6 and 1/2

265
00:11:09,760 --> 00:11:14,720
times.

266
00:11:11,279 --> 00:11:17,920
The same paper also reports 1.38 times

267
00:11:14,720 --> 00:11:20,959
throughput at a batch size of 64. Those

268
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:23,279
two numbers both describe Eagle 3. The 6

269
00:11:20,959 --> 00:11:27,680
and 1/2 is one request on an idle

270
00:11:23,279 --> 00:11:29,839
machine. The 1.38 is a machine with 64

271
00:11:27,680 --> 00:11:31,839
requests already competing for its

272
00:11:29,839 --> 00:11:33,920
compute.

273
00:11:31,839 --> 00:11:36,160
Look ahead decoding gets several tokens

274
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:38,800
out of a single pass with no drafter at

275
00:11:36,160 --> 00:11:41,279
all. It runs the model on several future

276
00:11:38,800 --> 00:11:44,160
positions at once, taking a rough guess

277
00:11:41,279 --> 00:11:45,839
at all of them. Then it runs again,

278
00:11:44,160 --> 00:11:49,519
improving the whole set of guesses

279
00:11:45,839 --> 00:11:51,839
together. On its own, that barely helps.

280
00:11:49,519 --> 00:11:54,160
A position only settles once everything

281
00:11:51,839 --> 00:11:56,800
before it has settled. So the sequence

282
00:11:54,160 --> 00:11:59,120
still fills in about one token at a time

283
00:11:56,800 --> 00:12:02,079
and the guesses that do land correctly

284
00:11:59,120 --> 00:12:03,839
get overwritten on the next round. But a

285
00:12:02,079 --> 00:12:06,320
guess in the wrong position is not the

286
00:12:03,839 --> 00:12:08,368
same as a wrong guess. A short run of

287
00:12:06,320 --> 00:12:10,320
tokens can sit in the wrong place and

288
00:12:08,368 --> 00:12:13,040
[music] still be a real continuation the

289
00:12:10,320 --> 00:12:16,320
model would produce somewhere. So look

290
00:12:13,040 --> 00:12:18,880
ahead keeps every one of them in a pool.

291
00:12:16,320 --> 00:12:20,639
Each time the model emits a real token,

292
00:12:18,880 --> 00:12:22,480
it pulls out the fragments that begin

293
00:12:20,639 --> 00:12:25,040
with that [music] token and checks them

294
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:27,600
in the same pass. The model ends up

295
00:12:25,040 --> 00:12:30,160
drafting for itself out of its own

296
00:12:27,600 --> 00:12:34,000
discarded work with nothing extra to

297
00:12:30,160 --> 00:12:36,320
train. The paper reports up to 1.8 times

298
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:39,120
on empty bench, which is a benchmark for

299
00:12:36,320 --> 00:12:42,320
evaluating LLMs in multi-turn dialogues

300
00:12:39,120 --> 00:12:44,399
with the output still exact.

301
00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:46,720
Every one of these gets more tokens out

302
00:12:44,399 --> 00:12:48,240
of a single read of the weights. The

303
00:12:46,720 --> 00:12:50,880
weights are not the only thing being

304
00:12:48,240 --> 00:12:52,720
read on every step though.

305
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:55,839
The other thing being read is the cache

306
00:12:52,720 --> 00:12:58,240
of keys and values. The model turns

307
00:12:55,839 --> 00:13:00,880
every token it reads into a key and a

308
00:12:58,240 --> 00:13:03,360
value and every later token needs those

309
00:13:00,880 --> 00:13:05,200
in order to attend to it. Storing them

310
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:08,639
saves the model from processing the

311
00:13:05,200 --> 00:13:10,880
whole context again at every step. At

312
00:13:08,639 --> 00:13:13,760
long context, that cache is a bigger

313
00:13:10,880 --> 00:13:16,000
problem than the weights. For a 70

314
00:13:13,760 --> 00:13:18,959
billion parameter model, that cache runs

315
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:22,000
about 320 kilobytes for every token of

316
00:13:18,959 --> 00:13:25,680
context. A single request stretched out

317
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:28,720
to 128,000 tokens is carrying roughly 40

318
00:13:25,680 --> 00:13:31,040
GB of cache on its own. Four requests

319
00:13:28,720 --> 00:13:33,600
that long hold more between them than

320
00:13:31,040 --> 00:13:36,720
the model holds in weights.

321
00:13:33,600 --> 00:13:39,519
And that cache does not just sit there.

322
00:13:36,720 --> 00:13:42,880
The GPU reads the whole cache back out

323
00:13:39,519 --> 00:13:44,720
of memory on every decode step. So those

324
00:13:42,880 --> 00:13:47,200
bytes compete with the weights for the

325
00:13:44,720 --> 00:13:48,880
same bandwidth.

326
00:13:47,200 --> 00:13:51,120
Flash attention already cut what

327
00:13:48,880 --> 00:13:53,680
attention has to move and it did that

328
00:13:51,120 --> 00:13:55,440
without changing a single output but it

329
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:57,519
never touched the size of the cache

330
00:13:55,440 --> 00:13:59,120
which still grows with every token that

331
00:13:57,519 --> 00:14:01,680
arrives.

332
00:13:59,120 --> 00:14:04,800
One way out is to keep every token and

333
00:14:01,680 --> 00:14:06,959
spread the cash across many devices.

334
00:14:04,800 --> 00:14:10,160
That is ring attention which we covered

335
00:14:06,959 --> 00:14:12,639
in episode 5. Each device holds a slice

336
00:14:10,160 --> 00:14:14,784
of the sequence and passes its block of

337
00:14:12,639 --> 00:14:17,760
keys and values around a ring until

338
00:14:14,784 --> 00:14:20,480
[music] every query has seen everyone.

339
00:14:17,760 --> 00:14:22,959
The result stays exact and what it needs

340
00:14:20,480 --> 00:14:24,880
is a set of devices with an interconnect

341
00:14:22,959 --> 00:14:27,199
fast enough to hide those transfers

342
00:14:24,880 --> 00:14:29,360
behind the arithmetic.

343
00:14:27,199 --> 00:14:31,920
The other ways we'll talk about next all

344
00:14:29,360 --> 00:14:34,480
give something up. You can make the

345
00:14:31,920 --> 00:14:37,040
model attend to fewer tokens, store

346
00:14:34,480 --> 00:14:40,079
fewer of them, or keep them out of the

347
00:14:37,040 --> 00:14:42,399
context in the first place. Each of the

348
00:14:40,079 --> 00:14:44,240
three can drop the very token the answer

349
00:14:42,399 --> 00:14:46,800
needed. In ring attention, [music]

350
00:14:44,240 --> 00:14:49,040
that token is still there.

351
00:14:46,800 --> 00:14:51,199
Attending to fewer tokens works because

352
00:14:49,040 --> 00:14:54,000
a trained model already ignores most of

353
00:14:51,199 --> 00:14:56,639
them. Most of the attention weight lands

354
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:58,720
on a handful of tokens. computing every

355
00:14:56,639 --> 00:15:01,600
pair anyway spends nearly all of the

356
00:14:58,720 --> 00:15:03,519
work on pairs that barely matter.

357
00:15:01,600 --> 00:15:06,480
The simplest version restricts each

358
00:15:03,519 --> 00:15:10,800
token to a fixed window of recent ones.

359
00:15:06,480 --> 00:15:13,519
Mistral 7B sets that window at 4,096

360
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:16,560
tokens. That looks like everything older

361
00:15:13,519 --> 00:15:19,120
is simply gone. It is not because the

362
00:15:16,560 --> 00:15:22,399
tokens inside that window have windows

363
00:15:19,120 --> 00:15:25,440
of their own. A token sees the 4,000

364
00:15:22,399 --> 00:15:28,880
positions behind it. Each of those has

365
00:15:25,440 --> 00:15:31,040
already seen the 4,000 behind itself. So

366
00:15:28,880 --> 00:15:33,760
after two layers, the newest token is

367
00:15:31,040 --> 00:15:36,320
carrying information from 8,000 back.

368
00:15:33,760 --> 00:15:38,959
After 32 layers that reaches about

369
00:15:36,320 --> 00:15:40,800
130,000 tokens in theory, even though no

370
00:15:38,959 --> 00:15:44,000
single layer ever looks further than

371
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:46,240
4,000. Once the window has moved past a

372
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:48,399
token, nothing will ever attend to it

373
00:15:46,240 --> 00:15:51,440
again. So its key and value can be

374
00:15:48,399 --> 00:15:54,880
thrown away. Each new token overwrites

375
00:15:51,440 --> 00:15:58,160
the entry 4,096 positions behind it, and

376
00:15:54,880 --> 00:16:00,560
the cash stops growing.

377
00:15:58,160 --> 00:16:02,720
Deepseek's native sparse attention makes

378
00:16:00,560 --> 00:16:04,240
the sparsity something the model learns

379
00:16:02,720 --> 00:16:05,759
rather than something bolted on

380
00:16:04,240 --> 00:16:08,480
afterwards.

381
00:16:05,759 --> 00:16:11,440
Every query runs three branches at once,

382
00:16:08,480 --> 00:16:13,519
each with its own keys and values. A

383
00:16:11,440 --> 00:16:16,560
compression branch cuts the older tokens

384
00:16:13,519 --> 00:16:19,600
into blocks of a fixed size and squashes

385
00:16:16,560 --> 00:16:21,279
each block into one course summary. A

386
00:16:19,600 --> 00:16:23,680
selection branch picks out a few

387
00:16:21,279 --> 00:16:26,399
important blocks and keeps them at full

388
00:16:23,680 --> 00:16:28,959
detail. A sliding branch handles

389
00:16:26,399 --> 00:16:30,959
whatever is nearby. Local patterns are

390
00:16:28,959 --> 00:16:33,040
the easiest thing to learn. So without

391
00:16:30,959 --> 00:16:36,000
it, the other two would learn those and

392
00:16:33,040 --> 00:16:39,040
not much else. A learned gate blends the

393
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:40,480
three outputs together because the model

394
00:16:39,040 --> 00:16:42,800
works out where to look during

395
00:16:40,480 --> 00:16:44,959
pre-training rather than after it.

396
00:16:42,800 --> 00:16:47,360
Native sparse attention matches or

397
00:16:44,959 --> 00:16:50,079
exceeds full attention across general

398
00:16:47,360 --> 00:16:53,120
benchmarks, long context tasks, and

399
00:16:50,079 --> 00:16:56,959
reasoning. It also runs substantially

400
00:16:53,120 --> 00:16:59,199
faster on 64,000 token sequences.

401
00:16:56,959 --> 00:17:02,079
Deepseek shipped a relative of it as

402
00:16:59,199 --> 00:17:04,160
deepseek sparse attention in v3.2

403
00:17:02,079 --> 00:17:06,799
experimental which [music] picks single

404
00:17:04,160 --> 00:17:09,280
tokens rather than whole blocks. They

405
00:17:06,799 --> 00:17:11,760
cut their API prices by more than half

406
00:17:09,280 --> 00:17:14,319
on the same day.

407
00:17:11,760 --> 00:17:17,120
Moonshot's mixture of block attention or

408
00:17:14,319 --> 00:17:20,480
MOA roots queries instead of compressing

409
00:17:17,120 --> 00:17:22,480
tokens. It cuts the context into blocks

410
00:17:20,480 --> 00:17:24,799
and puts a learned gate in front of

411
00:17:22,480 --> 00:17:27,520
them. So each query goes only to the

412
00:17:24,799 --> 00:17:30,320
blocks it needs. That is the mixture of

413
00:17:27,520 --> 00:17:32,240
experts idea from episode 6 applied to

414
00:17:30,320 --> 00:17:34,880
attention instead of to the feed forward

415
00:17:32,240 --> 00:17:37,200
network. The model decides where to

416
00:17:34,880 --> 00:17:40,080
attend instead of being handed a fixed

417
00:17:37,200 --> 00:17:42,720
pattern which MOBA's authors call a less

418
00:17:40,080 --> 00:17:44,880
structure principle. The same mechanism

419
00:17:42,720 --> 00:17:47,520
can fall back to full attention whenever

420
00:17:44,880 --> 00:17:50,880
it needs to. Moonshot deployed it to

421
00:17:47,520 --> 00:17:52,640
serve Kimy's long context requests.

422
00:17:50,880 --> 00:17:55,919
All of these change how much of the

423
00:17:52,640 --> 00:17:58,080
cache the GPU reads on each step. Apart

424
00:17:55,919 --> 00:18:00,960
from the rolling buffer, the tokens

425
00:17:58,080 --> 00:18:02,480
themselves are all still there.

426
00:18:00,960 --> 00:18:04,240
The other option is to delete them

427
00:18:02,480 --> 00:18:07,039
outright.

428
00:18:04,240 --> 00:18:09,520
Streaming LLM starts with the soft max,

429
00:18:07,039 --> 00:18:11,440
the step that decides how a new token

430
00:18:09,520 --> 00:18:14,000
spreads its attention across everything

431
00:18:11,440 --> 00:18:16,160
before it. Those weights have to add up

432
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:18,480
to one. So even when nothing in the

433
00:18:16,160 --> 00:18:21,679
context is a good match, that weight

434
00:18:18,480 --> 00:18:23,840
still has to land somewhere. Every token

435
00:18:21,679 --> 00:18:26,307
in the context ends up with a sliver of

436
00:18:23,840 --> 00:18:26,559
it, even the ones that match nothing.

437
00:18:26,307 --> 00:18:28,559
>> [music]

438
00:18:26,559 --> 00:18:30,640
>> The model resolves this by dumping the

439
00:18:28,559 --> 00:18:33,679
leftover onto the first few tokens of

440
00:18:30,640 --> 00:18:36,720
the sequence. Those tokens work because

441
00:18:33,679 --> 00:18:38,799
every later position can see them.

442
00:18:36,720 --> 00:18:40,559
Streaming LLM keeps those sync tokens

443
00:18:38,799 --> 00:18:43,679
alongside a rolling window of recent

444
00:18:40,559 --> 00:18:45,520
ones and evicts everything in between. A

445
00:18:43,679 --> 00:18:47,679
model trained on a finite window can

446
00:18:45,520 --> 00:18:50,720
then keep generating over a stream of 4

447
00:18:47,679 --> 00:18:53,120
million tokens and more. That is not a 4

448
00:18:50,720 --> 00:18:55,440
million token memory. The window never

449
00:18:53,120 --> 00:18:58,000
grows and whatever falls out of it is

450
00:18:55,440 --> 00:19:01,280
gone permanently.

451
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:04,240
H2O, the heavy hitter oracle, is a paper

452
00:19:01,280 --> 00:19:06,799
from 2023 with a second way to choose

453
00:19:04,240 --> 00:19:08,960
what to evict. It keeps tokens by

454
00:19:06,799 --> 00:19:11,840
attention received rather than by

455
00:19:08,960 --> 00:19:13,360
position. A small fraction of tokens

456
00:19:11,840 --> 00:19:16,720
account for most of the attention

457
00:19:13,360 --> 00:19:20,160
weight. H2O keeps those alongside recent

458
00:19:16,720 --> 00:19:23,280
ones and runs on about 20% of the cache.

459
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:25,200
Snap KV applies the same idea earlier,

460
00:19:23,280 --> 00:19:28,080
compressing the prompts cache before

461
00:19:25,200 --> 00:19:29,840
generation even starts. Both estimate

462
00:19:28,080 --> 00:19:31,039
importance from attention that has

463
00:19:29,840 --> 00:19:33,440
already happened. [music]

464
00:19:31,039 --> 00:19:36,640
So both can discard a token that only

465
00:19:33,440 --> 00:19:39,280
becomes relevant later. Quantizing the

466
00:19:36,640 --> 00:19:42,720
cash instead of evicting it keeps every

467
00:19:39,280 --> 00:19:45,440
token and shrinks each one. Episode 4

468
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:49,919
covered that in detail.

469
00:19:45,440 --> 00:19:52,160
A 2025 study called the sparse frontier

470
00:19:49,919 --> 00:19:55,120
measured what all of this does to

471
00:19:52,160 --> 00:19:58,320
accuracy and found that how much you can

472
00:19:55,120 --> 00:20:01,760
throw away varies dramatically from task

473
00:19:58,320 --> 00:20:04,559
to task. A question that needs one fact

474
00:20:01,760 --> 00:20:07,919
buried in the context survives even when

475
00:20:04,559 --> 00:20:09,520
95% of the attention is thrown away. A

476
00:20:07,919 --> 00:20:12,320
question that needs several facts

477
00:20:09,520 --> 00:20:14,400
assembled starts failing at 80. No

478
00:20:12,320 --> 00:20:17,440
method wins everywhere and the right

479
00:20:14,400 --> 00:20:19,600
choice depends on the task. When it does

480
00:20:17,440 --> 00:20:21,919
go wrong, the answer still comes back

481
00:20:19,600 --> 00:20:23,760
fluent with the missing fact quietly

482
00:20:21,919 --> 00:20:25,840
replaced.

483
00:20:23,760 --> 00:20:28,159
Retrieval augmented generation keeps the

484
00:20:25,840 --> 00:20:30,240
documents outside the prompt and pulls

485
00:20:28,159 --> 00:20:33,440
in only the few pieces each question

486
00:20:30,240 --> 00:20:36,240
needs. The corpus is everything you want

487
00:20:33,440 --> 00:20:38,799
the model to be able to draw on. It gets

488
00:20:36,240 --> 00:20:41,360
split into chunks and indexed ahead of

489
00:20:38,799 --> 00:20:44,480
time. Each chunk is stored as a vector

490
00:20:41,360 --> 00:20:46,400
that captures roughly what it says. When

491
00:20:44,480 --> 00:20:48,640
a question arrives, the system turns the

492
00:20:46,400 --> 00:20:51,039
question into a vector, too. It finds

493
00:20:48,640 --> 00:20:53,919
the chunks nearest to it and puts only

494
00:20:51,039 --> 00:20:55,760
those few in the prompt. The context

495
00:20:53,919 --> 00:20:58,240
stays a few thousand tokens long, no

496
00:20:55,760 --> 00:21:01,039
matter how large the corpus is, so the

497
00:20:58,240 --> 00:21:04,320
cash never gets a chance to grow.

498
00:21:01,039 --> 00:21:07,120
A 2020 paper introduced this using a

499
00:21:04,320 --> 00:21:09,840
dense vector index of Wikipedia with a

500
00:21:07,120 --> 00:21:12,000
trained retriever in front of it. Saving

501
00:21:09,840 --> 00:21:14,400
memory was not what its authors were

502
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:17,120
after. They wanted a model that could

503
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:19,360
reach knowledge it had never memorized.

504
00:21:17,120 --> 00:21:23,039
Keeping the context short is a side

505
00:21:19,360 --> 00:21:25,520
effect that turned out to matter later.

506
00:21:23,039 --> 00:21:27,919
In exchange, the retriever now decides

507
00:21:25,520 --> 00:21:29,840
what the model is allowed to know. If

508
00:21:27,919 --> 00:21:33,120
the retriever misses the chunk holding

509
00:21:29,840 --> 00:21:35,600
the answer, the model never sees it. No

510
00:21:33,120 --> 00:21:38,400
amount of capability recovers a document

511
00:21:35,600 --> 00:21:40,400
that was never put in front of it. You

512
00:21:38,400 --> 00:21:42,720
are also running a second system now

513
00:21:40,400 --> 00:21:46,080
with its own index to build, refresh,

514
00:21:42,720 --> 00:21:49,120
and tune. It fits badly with prefix

515
00:21:46,080 --> 00:21:52,080
caching, too. That technique that we

516
00:21:49,120 --> 00:21:54,240
discussed in episode 7 works because an

517
00:21:52,080 --> 00:21:56,880
identical run of tokens produces

518
00:21:54,240 --> 00:21:59,520
identical keys and values. So the shared

519
00:21:56,880 --> 00:22:02,240
front of a prompt can be computed once

520
00:21:59,520 --> 00:22:04,640
and reused. Retrieve chunks change from

521
00:22:02,240 --> 00:22:07,280
query to query and they sit near the

522
00:22:04,640 --> 00:22:09,600
front of the prompt. Two requests

523
00:22:07,280 --> 00:22:12,080
diverge almost immediately and there is

524
00:22:09,600 --> 00:22:14,159
little left to share. Nothing about them

525
00:22:12,080 --> 00:22:17,360
tells you whether the service built on

526
00:22:14,159 --> 00:22:19,760
top is meeting anyone's deadline.

527
00:22:17,360 --> 00:22:21,600
Two numbers decide whether a service is

528
00:22:19,760 --> 00:22:24,559
hitting its deadlines [music] and we

529
00:22:21,600 --> 00:22:26,799
covered both of them in episode 1. Time

530
00:22:24,559 --> 00:22:28,159
to first token is prefill plus however

531
00:22:26,799 --> 00:22:30,880
long the request sat in [music] the

532
00:22:28,159 --> 00:22:33,120
queue. Time per output token comes from

533
00:22:30,880 --> 00:22:34,799
memory bound decode.

534
00:22:33,120 --> 00:22:37,520
In production, we do not watch the

535
00:22:34,799 --> 00:22:39,840
average of either. We watch the tail,

536
00:22:37,520 --> 00:22:42,799
the 99th percentile, which is the

537
00:22:39,840 --> 00:22:45,600
slowest one request in a 100. That is

538
00:22:42,799 --> 00:22:47,840
the request a user remembers and the one

539
00:22:45,600 --> 00:22:50,400
that decides whether the service met the

540
00:22:47,840 --> 00:22:53,360
latency it promised.

541
00:22:50,400 --> 00:22:55,600
Sizing the fleet works the same way. A

542
00:22:53,360 --> 00:22:57,760
fleet sized for the mean fails [music]

543
00:22:55,600 --> 00:23:00,480
at the peaks which is when the most

544
00:22:57,760 --> 00:23:04,080
people are using it. So you build

545
00:23:00,480 --> 00:23:06,320
against the load at the 99th percentile.

546
00:23:04,080 --> 00:23:09,200
GPU utilization is the figure most

547
00:23:06,320 --> 00:23:11,200
dashboards lead with. Nvidia's own

548
00:23:09,200 --> 00:23:14,159
definition is the percentage of time

549
00:23:11,200 --> 00:23:17,120
over a sample period during which one or

550
00:23:14,159 --> 00:23:19,200
more kernels was executing. That tells

551
00:23:17,120 --> 00:23:21,520
you whether the chip is busy, not

552
00:23:19,200 --> 00:23:23,840
whether it is getting anything done. A

553
00:23:21,520 --> 00:23:26,400
decode stepalled on memory leaves the

554
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:29,039
arithmetic units nearly idle, but it is

555
00:23:26,400 --> 00:23:31,919
still a kernel executing. So utilization

556
00:23:29,039 --> 00:23:34,000
reports 100%.

557
00:23:31,919 --> 00:23:36,320
So the numbers worth tracking are the

558
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:38,400
ones that actually move. [music] Tokens

559
00:23:36,320 --> 00:23:41,120
per second is the product itself.

560
00:23:38,400 --> 00:23:43,440
Batchfulness counts how many requests

561
00:23:41,120 --> 00:23:45,520
share each weight read and Q weight

562
00:23:43,440 --> 00:23:47,840
climbs before anything else when

563
00:23:45,520 --> 00:23:49,840
capacity runs short.

564
00:23:47,840 --> 00:23:51,679
agents work differently, running the

565
00:23:49,840 --> 00:23:54,000
model over and over and feeding the

566
00:23:51,679 --> 00:23:57,360
whole history back in each time. The

567
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:59,679
Manis team said the KV cache hit rate is

568
00:23:57,360 --> 00:24:02,080
the single most important metric for a

569
00:23:59,679 --> 00:24:06,240
production agent because it affects both

570
00:24:02,080 --> 00:24:09,039
latency and cost. An agent reads about

571
00:24:06,240 --> 00:24:12,960
100 tokens of history for every token it

572
00:24:09,039 --> 00:24:15,840
generates. In episode 7, we priced a

573
00:24:12,960 --> 00:24:19,919
cached read at a tenth of an uncashed

574
00:24:15,840 --> 00:24:23,360
one. So, at a 90% hit rate, those 100

575
00:24:19,919 --> 00:24:25,200
reads come out at 19.

576
00:24:23,360 --> 00:24:27,440
None of these numbers say anything about

577
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:28,960
money. That comes from dividing what the

578
00:24:27,440 --> 00:24:30,799
machine costs by what it actually

579
00:24:28,960 --> 00:24:33,120
produced.

580
00:24:30,799 --> 00:24:36,159
The cost of a million tokens is what you

581
00:24:33,120 --> 00:24:38,480
rent the GPU for in an hour divided by

582
00:24:36,159 --> 00:24:42,159
the tokens it produced in that hour.

583
00:24:38,480 --> 00:24:44,000
Renting an H100 runs about $3 an hour,

584
00:24:42,159 --> 00:24:46,880
though across providers that spans

585
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:49,360
anywhere from 1.5 to8.

586
00:24:46,880 --> 00:24:52,240
At the low end of utilization, Nvidia

587
00:24:49,360 --> 00:24:55,679
measured Llama 3.370B

588
00:24:52,240 --> 00:24:59,200
on a single H200 one request at a time

589
00:24:55,679 --> 00:25:04,000
at 51 output tokens a second over an

590
00:24:59,200 --> 00:25:07,840
hour. That is 184,000 tokens. $3 divided

591
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:09,919
by 184,000 comes to about $16 per

592
00:25:07,840 --> 00:25:11,679
million tokens.

593
00:25:09,919 --> 00:25:13,360
Filling the machine changes that number

594
00:25:11,679 --> 00:25:17,360
completely.

595
00:25:13,360 --> 00:25:22,559
Perplexity measured Llama 270B on two

596
00:25:17,360 --> 00:25:24,720
H100s running 128 requests at once. That

597
00:25:22,559 --> 00:25:28,799
came to 767

598
00:25:24,720 --> 00:25:32,240
output tokens a second per GPU. That is

599
00:25:28,799 --> 00:25:35,600
2.76 million tokens an hour. So the same

600
00:25:32,240 --> 00:25:37,520
$3 now works out to about $19 per

601
00:25:35,600 --> 00:25:40,400
million.

602
00:25:37,520 --> 00:25:43,200
That is $16 against one on the same

603
00:25:40,400 --> 00:25:46,240
hourly rate and the same model size

604
00:25:43,200 --> 00:25:48,720
roughly 15 times apart. Nothing about

605
00:25:46,240 --> 00:25:50,720
the chip changed. All that changed is

606
00:25:48,720 --> 00:25:52,559
how many requests were sharing each read

607
00:25:50,720 --> 00:25:54,480
of the weights.

608
00:25:52,559 --> 00:25:56,559
Those two measurements come from

609
00:25:54,480 --> 00:25:59,600
different machines and different prompt

610
00:25:56,559 --> 00:26:02,080
lengths and both are vendor best cases.

611
00:25:59,600 --> 00:26:05,039
So the dollar figures are illustrative.

612
00:26:02,080 --> 00:26:08,640
The ratio is the part that holds.

613
00:26:05,039 --> 00:26:11,039
It also settles which decision matters.

614
00:26:08,640 --> 00:26:12,880
What you pay per GPU hour varies by two

615
00:26:11,039 --> 00:26:15,279
or three times between comparable

616
00:26:12,880 --> 00:26:17,600
providers. What you get out of that hour

617
00:26:15,279 --> 00:26:20,799
varies by more than 10. Choosing a

618
00:26:17,600 --> 00:26:22,559
cheaper chip is the small optimization.

619
00:26:20,799 --> 00:26:24,960
The denominator needs one correction

620
00:26:22,559 --> 00:26:26,880
though. A token generated after its

621
00:26:24,960 --> 00:26:29,360
deadline has already used the compute

622
00:26:26,880 --> 00:26:31,200
and it cannot be sold. So the tokens

623
00:26:29,360 --> 00:26:33,679
that count are the ones delivered inside

624
00:26:31,200 --> 00:26:36,240
their latency targets.

625
00:26:33,679 --> 00:26:38,000
That is goodput and goodput per dollar

626
00:26:36,240 --> 00:26:39,600
is what the whole stack is arranged

627
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:41,919
around.

628
00:26:39,600 --> 00:26:44,640
The workload itself is changing and the

629
00:26:41,919 --> 00:26:46,559
change makes decode matter more.

630
00:26:44,640 --> 00:26:49,279
Reasoning models think before they

631
00:26:46,559 --> 00:26:51,039
answer producing a long internal chain

632
00:26:49,279 --> 00:26:54,000
of thought that is not part of the

633
00:26:51,039 --> 00:26:56,400
answer. That is a few hundred tokens for

634
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:58,240
an easy question and tens of thousands

635
00:26:56,400 --> 00:27:00,480
for a hard one.

636
00:26:58,240 --> 00:27:03,279
Every one of those thinking tokens is a

637
00:27:00,480 --> 00:27:05,760
decode step. Each one is memory bound

638
00:27:03,279 --> 00:27:08,159
and takes a full read of the weights.

639
00:27:05,760 --> 00:27:11,039
All of it happens before the user has

640
00:27:08,159 --> 00:27:13,120
seen a single word of the answer. The

641
00:27:11,039 --> 00:27:16,080
longer a model reasons, the more of the

642
00:27:13,120 --> 00:27:18,559
work in one answer is decoded.

643
00:27:16,080 --> 00:27:20,640
The same shift shows up in training too.

644
00:27:18,559 --> 00:27:22,799
The reinforcement learning that produces

645
00:27:20,640 --> 00:27:25,120
reasoning models is dominated by

646
00:27:22,799 --> 00:27:27,679
rollouts where the model generates its

647
00:27:25,120 --> 00:27:30,640
own attempts and they get scored.

648
00:27:27,679 --> 00:27:33,039
And a roll out is just inference. The

649
00:27:30,640 --> 00:27:36,080
Rhyme RL team profiled this with

650
00:27:33,039 --> 00:27:38,799
responses capped at 16,000 tokens.

651
00:27:36,080 --> 00:27:40,880
Rollouts took 91% of an entire

652
00:27:38,799 --> 00:27:43,064
reinforcement learning step for a math

653
00:27:40,880 --> 00:27:44,640
model. For a code model, it was 84%.

654
00:27:43,064 --> 00:27:49,200
[music]

655
00:27:44,640 --> 00:27:51,440
At 32,000 tokens, both pass 95%.

656
00:27:49,200 --> 00:27:54,399
Their explanation is the one this series

657
00:27:51,440 --> 00:27:57,039
started with. Generating each token

658
00:27:54,399 --> 00:27:59,760
requires a forward pass and that pass is

659
00:27:57,039 --> 00:28:02,059
constrained by memory bandwidth.

660
00:27:59,760 --> 00:28:05,120
Training a reasoning model is now mostly

661
00:28:02,059 --> 00:28:07,520
[music] an inference workload.

662
00:28:05,120 --> 00:28:10,159
Speculative decoding got several tokens

663
00:28:07,520 --> 00:28:11,760
out of a single weight read. Ring

664
00:28:10,159 --> 00:28:14,159
attention spread the cash across

665
00:28:11,760 --> 00:28:16,399
devices. Sparse attention read less of

666
00:28:14,159 --> 00:28:18,640
it each step. Cash eviction deleted

667
00:28:16,399 --> 00:28:20,640
parts of it outright. And retrieval kept

668
00:28:18,640 --> 00:28:23,760
the context short enough that it never

669
00:28:20,640 --> 00:28:26,640
grew. Goodput decides whether any of

670
00:28:23,760 --> 00:28:29,279
that arrived inside its deadline.

671
00:28:26,640 --> 00:28:32,960
The Stanford AI index tracked the price

672
00:28:29,279 --> 00:28:36,880
of a GPT 3.5 level score on the standard

673
00:28:32,960 --> 00:28:39,840
knowledge benchmark. In November 2022,

674
00:28:36,880 --> 00:28:43,679
that was $20 per million tokens. By

675
00:28:39,840 --> 00:28:45,944
October 2024, it was 7.

676
00:28:43,679 --> 00:28:48,880
That is more than 280 times cheaper

677
00:28:45,944 --> 00:28:51,120
[music] in under 2 years. The capability

678
00:28:48,880 --> 00:28:54,399
is held fixed and only the model

679
00:28:51,120 --> 00:28:56,640
changes. An 8 billion parameter model

680
00:28:54,399 --> 00:28:59,039
reached that score running on a stack

681
00:28:56,640 --> 00:29:01,360
built over 3 years to move fewer bytes

682
00:28:59,039 --> 00:29:04,720
per token.

683
00:29:01,360 --> 00:29:07,360
The hardware did not stand still either.

684
00:29:04,720 --> 00:29:10,720
Memory bandwidth roughly quadrupled over

685
00:29:07,360 --> 00:29:14,240
4 years from about 2 tab a second on the

686
00:29:10,720 --> 00:29:16,720
A100 to around 8 on [music] Blackwell.

687
00:29:14,240 --> 00:29:19,360
Compute climbed faster still. So the gap

688
00:29:16,720 --> 00:29:21,760
that decode falls into is wider now than

689
00:29:19,360 --> 00:29:24,320
when this series started.

690
00:29:21,760 --> 00:29:26,720
Every technique in these eight episodes

691
00:29:24,320 --> 00:29:28,880
is a way of getting more useful work out

692
00:29:26,720 --> 00:29:30,399
of every bite that crosses the memory

693
00:29:28,880 --> 00:29:32,799
bus.

694
00:29:30,399 --> 00:29:34,720
Each arrived on its own answering its

695
00:29:32,799 --> 00:29:37,520
own bottleneck and we have discussed

696
00:29:34,720 --> 00:29:40,000
them one at a time. A request arriving

697
00:29:37,520 --> 00:29:42,720
in production meets all of them at once

698
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:45,200
in a single pass.

699
00:29:42,720 --> 00:29:47,279
The last episode in this LLM inference

700
00:29:45,200 --> 00:29:51,039
engineering series follows one real

701
00:29:47,279 --> 00:29:53,039
request into a rack of 72 GPUs. It goes

702
00:29:51,039 --> 00:29:55,039
from the routing decision that picks its

703
00:29:53,039 --> 00:29:58,039
machine to the last token it streams

704
00:29:55,039 --> 00:29:58,039
back.

